// THE SAME REFUSAL, WITH THE BOUND WRITTEN ABOVE THE TRAIT IT NAMES.
//
// This file is `trait_ident_homonym_bound_refused.logos` with ONE difference:
// `fn hmid_tag<T: Hash>` is written BEFORE `pub trait Hash`. Nothing else moves
// — same package, same single TU, same stdlib `Hash` owning the bare registry
// slot, same expectation string.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. B-mv-03 captures a bound's trait IDENTITY at the site the
// bound is WRITTEN (SemaChecker::read_trait_bound_args → canonical_trait_name).
// That capture reads `traits_`, and `traits_` is filled in item order, so a
// forward reference used to resolve against a registry that did not yet contain
// the local trait. MEASURED on the merged tree before the pass-0 fix:
//   · trait first (the sibling file): refused at the bound, with the ground.
//   · fn first  (this file):          NOT refused — full sema, full mono, full
//     mlir-gen, then `error: 'func.call' op 'i64__tag' does not reference a
//     valid function`, rc 1. Exactly the pre-B-mv-03 defect, back purely
//     because of the order two items are written in.
//
// ⚠ WHY A GREEN CORPUS COULD NEVER HAVE FOUND THIS. The mis-resolution is
// always PERMISSIVE: an unresolved bound falls back to the bare spelling, which
// admits the OTHER homonym's impls. A bound that admits too much never turns a
// working program red, so nothing in a passing suite is disturbed by it — the
// hole is only visible from a program that MUST be refused and is not. That is
// why the fix lives at the registration (pass-0 pre-registers the trait under
// the key collect_trait will use) and not in a downstream sensor.
//
// ⚠ WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT PIN. Not the MLIR verifier message above — that is
// the DEFECT, and a `fail/` door holding it would be a green test asserting a
// defect. `.expected` carries the sema refusal and its ground, which the
// verifier failure cannot satisfy: it never names a trait at all.
//
// LINK ORDER, stated as ground exactly as the sibling states it: single TU, no
// `-l` archive, so the stdlib's `logos.lang.hash::Hash` is collected FIRST and
// owns the bare slot; THIS package's `Hash` lives under its qualified key. The
// mirror link order (an archive's homonym owning the bare slot) is NOT closed
// by either file — see the sibling's closing note.
//
// The legitimate direction is exercised in this same file: `hmid_tag(S{..})`
// must still bind through the package-local `Hash`. If the fix had refused
// every `Hash` bound instead of separating the two traits, the first call would
// be the one that failed, and the ground below would not be printed for it.
package trait_ident_homonym_bound_refused;

extern fn printf(fmt: *const u8, ...) -> i32;

// ⚠ WRITTEN ABOVE THE TRAIT ON PURPOSE. Do not "tidy" this by moving it below
// the trait declaration — that is the sibling file, and it asks a question this
// one does not.
fn hmid_tag<T: Hash>(x: T) -> i64 { return x.tag(); }

pub trait Hash { fn tag(&self) -> i64; }

pub struct S { pub a: i32, }
impl Hash for S { fn tag(&self) -> i64 { return 91i64; } }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let ok: i64 = hmid_tag(S { a: 1i32 });   // the legitimate direction
    let bad: i64 = hmid_tag(5i64);           // i64 implements the STDLIB Hash
    if ok != 91i64 { return 41; }
    if bad != 91i64 { return 42; }
    return 0i32;
}
